// botanicals.mjs — research-grade botanical monographs for the MELEK hemp/cannabinoid science shelf.
//
// This shelf holds deep monographs on the non-cannabis botanicals that touch cannabinoid and
// endocannabinoid pharmacology, liver metabolism, or the Van Kush Family Research Institute's
// working materia medica: Imphepho (South African Helichrysum), Yin Chen Hao (Artemisia
// capillaris), Mucuna pruriens, kava, black pepper, hops, maca, echinacea and Acmella oleracea.
// WRITTEN SO FAR: Imphepho (Helichrysum odoratissimum / petiolare / cymosum), Yin Chen Hao
// (Artemisia capillaris) and Mucuna pruriens. The kava, black pepper, hops, maca, echinacea and
// Acmella monographs named above are PLANNED AND NOT YET WRITTEN — they are not on this shelf, and
// the shelf page reports the pages it actually has rather than the ones it intends to have. Their
// interaction roles ARE already modelled in integrations/interactions-cannabis.mjs, which is a
// separate dataset and does not depend on a monograph existing here.
//
// Each page carries taxonomy, phytochemistry with real chemotype numbers, pharmacology by named
// mechanism, traditional use attributed to the culture that holds it, preparation-relevant
// CHEMISTRY (solubility, volatility, thermal and oxidative lability), documented interactions with
// the mechanism named, and an explicit statement of what is not known.
//
// Source material: the operator's Temple Pharmacopoeia knowledgebase (sections 3-6) and the Kava
// Potentiators datasheet, cross-checked against the primary literature. Where the operator's
// material carried a claim we could not support, the claim is marked contested and the correction
// is stated in the body rather than silently dropped.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CONTAIN: preparative or synthetic procedures of any kind.
// No extraction protocols, no solvent systems, no ratios, no temperatures, no reaction times, no
// work-ups, no yields-of-process, no distillation parameters, no formulation recipes. Where the
// literature (or the operator's own source document) contains such a procedure, this file states
// only that the procedure exists and cites it. Solubility, volatility and degradation chemistry
// ARE included, because they are properties of the compound, not instructions for making anything.
//
// Nothing here is medical advice. It is education and harm reduction: what is documented, by whom,
// at what evidence grade, and where the evidence stops.

export const SHELF = Object.freeze({
  id: 'botanicals',
  title: 'Botanical Monographs',
  blurb: 'Research-grade monographs on the botanicals that touch cannabinoid pharmacology, liver metabolism and the temple materia medica — chemotype data, mechanism-level pharmacology, and interactions with the mechanism named.',
  updated: '2026-09-27',
});

